course: dictionary
lesson: 1859
title: Words with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them as 1) and 2). To choose the right one, look at the context, meaning the other words in the sentence. «Picture a small church in Wellington: at the front stands a long wooden box covered in flowers, the coffin.» The church and the flowers show that a coffin is the box for a dead person.
explain: Context can also show when a word belongs to a phrase. A phrase is a group of words whose meaning is not just the meaning of each word. «A nail in the coffin» is an idiom. It does not mean a real nail in a real box. It means something that helps to end or ruin something. You must read the whole phrase to understand it.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Try «a long box for a dead person» in the church sentence and it fits. Try it in «a nail in the coffin» and it does not, so the idiom has its own meaning.
fact: A coffin is a long box in which a dead person is buried or cremated.
fact: In a burial, «the coffin is lowered into a grave» in a cemetery or churchyard.
fact: In Cork, Granda's wake is a gathering at home to remember the person, often with the coffin there. People tell funny stories, like «He was some craic», which is Irish for great fun.
fact: Egyptian coffins, called sarcophagi, appear in history lessons alongside mummies.
fact: In spooky stories, vampires sleep in coffins.
exercise: «Picture a small church in Wellington: at the front stands a long wooden box covered in flowers, the coffin.» What is a coffin in this sentence? | *A long box for a dead person | A church bell | A table for the flowers | A type of flower
exercise: Leo thinks «a nail in the coffin» is about a real nail. What does the phrase mean? | A sad song | A real nail hammered into a wooden box | A wooden box at the front of a church | *Something that helps to end or ruin something
exercise: «He was some craic» is said at Granda's wake. What does craic mean here? | Very rude behaviour | Very cold weather | *Great fun | A kind of wooden box
test: «Mourners often shovel earth into the grave themselves.» What is a grave? | A small hill covered in grass | *A place where a dead person is buried | A large bunch of flowers | A church bell tower
test: Egyptian coffins are called what? | *Sarcophagi | Cemeteries | Graves | Churchyards
exam: What does the dictionary's label «(idiom)» tell you about «a nail in the coffin»? | It is a word for a very long box | It is only used in churches | It is a word that is spelled wrongly | *It is a phrase whose meaning is more than its single words
